.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the core process of FuguPass.
It holds the vault, the one-shot subcommands, and the ceremonies.
One vault is one directory of files.
.Pp
.Nm
reads each passphrase from the terminal.
It runs each helper program as a child process, and text alone
crosses that boundary.
.Pp
A run with no subcommand starts an interactive session.
.Nm
opens the session, and it then spawns
.Xr fugupass-repl 1
as a child process, with a request pipe and a reply pipe.
That child reads each operator command line and shows the non-secret
output, and
.Nm
runs each command.
No secret crosses the two pipes.
.Pp
The session locks on the command
.Cm quit ,
at the end of the request pipe, and after an idle time with no
request.
The lock erases every session secret, and the closed reply pipe ends
the child.
The config file holds the idle time as
.Ic lock-timeout ,
and the default is 300 seconds.
.Pp
Every subcommand but
.Cm create ,
.Cm passwd ,
.Cm resume ,
.Cm refill ,
.Cm provision ,
.Cm kit ,
.Cm revoke ,
.Cm recover
and
.Cm verify
opens a session of the vault first.
The session reads the passphrase once.
It takes the quorum of
.Ar threshold
oracles from the reachable oracles, in the order of the config file.
It prefers the oracles that hold a live index wrap on this machine.
It then verifies the passphrase at the canary record of each quorum
oracle, one oracle at a time, and it stops at the first failure.
The same answers open the index of the vault.
.Pp
A reveal of one entry sends one request to each quorum oracle.
The session unmasks one share of each answer, it reconstructs the
key of the entry, and it opens the entry file of that key.
A failure at one oracle moves that oracle out of the quorum, and the
next reachable oracle takes the place.
Each reveal substitutes each reachable oracle at most once, and it
stops when no untried reachable oracle remains.
One record is one slot at one oracle, and one session sends two
requests at most to one record.
The third wrong request destroys the key material of that record at
the oracle.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d Ar directory
Take
.Ar directory
as the vault directory.
The default is
.Pa .fugupass
of the home directory.
.Nm
makes the directory when no directory of that path exists.
.El
.Pp
The subcommands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Cm create
.Fl k Ar threshold
.Fl m Ar machine
.Fl r Ar rounds
.Op Fl p Ar slots
.Ar oracle ...
.Xc
Make a new vault on this machine.
The ceremony takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 .
It writes the device factor and the config file, and it reads the
passphrase.
It then enrolls a canary record at each oracle, and it fills the
pool of slots.
Each slot takes one record at each oracle.
The ceremony ends with the index.
.Pp
Each
.Ar oracle
argument holds one position of the ordered oracle set: the static
public key hex, one space, then the URL.
The first argument is position 1.
The three options below are mandatory:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k Ar threshold
The count of oracles that one reveal needs.
The value must not exceed the count of the
.Ar oracle
arguments.
.It Fl m Ar machine
The name of this machine.
The name holds lowercase ASCII letters, digits, and hyphens, 1 to 64
bytes.
The config file records it.
.It Fl r Ar rounds
The round count of
.Xr bcrypt_pbkdf 3 .
.El
.Pp
The option below is optional:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p Ar slots
The count of slots of the new pool.
The default is 64 slots.
The ceremony derives each slot, so a large pool takes a long
ceremony and many oracle requests.
The config file records the value as
.Ic pool-size .
.El
.Pp
The ceremony reads the passphrase twice, at the prompt
.Dq Passphrase:
and at the prompt
.Dq Passphrase again: .
The two reads must match.
No file of the vault verifies this passphrase, so the ceremony warns
before the first prompt.
.Pp
The ceremony writes these files of the vault directory: the device
factor, the config file, the counters file, one canary seal of each
oracle, and one index wrap of each oracle.
It writes one sealed file of each slot of the pool, one wrap of each
slot at each oracle, and the index.
.It Cm canary Ar oracle
Enroll the canary record of one oracle again.
.Ar oracle
is the position of that oracle in the ordered set.
The unlock of the session verifies the current passphrase first, so
a wiped canary of another oracle stops this subcommand.
The enrollment takes two reads of the passphrase, and the unlock
took the first one.
The subcommand therefore reads the same passphrase once more, and it
enrolls the record under a fresh canary mask.
It proves the fresh record with one immediate request, and it writes
the canary seal of that oracle.
.Pp
The re-enrollment kills this machine's index wrap of that oracle.
A session that holds the index key wraps the index share again at
once.
A session without that key deletes the dead wrap file, and it names
the
.Cm provision
subcommand.
.It Cm passwd
Change the passphrase of this machine.
The change re-enrolls every record of this machine at every live
oracle, and it takes no master.
Each other machine keeps the old passphrase on its own records,
until the change runs on that machine.
.Pp
The subcommand reads the old passphrase twice, at the prompts
.Dq Old passphrase:
and
.Dq Old passphrase again: .
It reads the new passphrase twice, at the prompts
.Dq New passphrase:
and
.Dq New passphrase again: .
The two reads of each passphrase must match.
A canary enrollment of the change can take the old passphrase.
That enrollment needs the two reads of it.
.Pp
The change then verifies the old passphrase at the canary record of
each live oracle.
It re-enrolls a canary that fails for record-side causes, after the
check of the reachable oracles.
A mistyped old passphrase stops the change at the first canary of
that passphrase, and no record of the vault changes.
A change that starts with fewer than the threshold of reachable
oracles stops before the first record, and it writes no marker.
.Pp
The change takes each slot in turn, and each live oracle of a slot
in the order of the config file.
It opens the file of a slot through the quorum before the first
record of that slot.
A file that no quorum opens stops the change there, and the report
names the slot and each quorum.
The canary records come last.
.Pp
The change writes the change marker before the first record, and it
removes the marker after the last one.
While the marker exists, a session refuses each reveal, and the
report names the
.Cm resume
subcommand.
An unreachable live oracle leaves the change incomplete, and the
marker stays.
.It Cm resume
Complete the passphrase change that the change marker holds.
The subcommand reads the two passphrases again, as
.Cm passwd
reads them.
It takes the new passphrase for every record of the marker, and the
old passphrase for every other record.
It verifies the new passphrase at each canary of the marker, and the
old passphrase at every other canary.
The change resumes at the first record that the marker does not
hold, and it removes the marker after the last record.
.Pp
A vault that holds no marker gives a report and the exit status 1.
A marker of a threshold change gives a report and the exit status 1,
and the report names the
.Cm provision
subcommand.
.It Cm refill
Add slots to the pool of this vault.
The ceremony takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 ,
as
.Cm create
takes it.
It reads the passphrase once, and it verifies that passphrase at the
canary record of each live oracle before the first new slot.
.Pp
The subcommand takes no option.
The count of the new slots is the
.Ic pool-size
line of the config file, and a file with no such line gives 64
slots.
The new slots take the slot indexes after the pool, so the ceremony
writes no file of an existing entry.
.Pp
The index takes the new pool state after the last new slot.
An interrupted refill therefore leaves the index as it was, and a
second run is safe.
The ceremony refuses to start while the change marker exists, and
the report names the
.Cm resume
subcommand.
.It Xo
.Cm provision
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl x Ar position
.Fl k Ar threshold
.Fl m Ar machine
.Fl r Ar rounds
.Ar oracle ...
.Xc
Add this machine to an existing vault.
Copy the shared set of the vault into the vault directory first:
the index, and each entry file.
Any transport serves, because every file of the set is ciphertext.
The ceremony takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 ,
and it opens the index under the index key of that plate.
It writes the device factor and the config file, and it reads the
passphrase twice, as
.Cm create
reads it.
It then enrolls a canary record of this machine at each live oracle.
For each slot of the vault, it enrolls one record of this machine at
each live oracle, and it writes the wrap of that record.
It registers the machine name in the index last.
Every entry then reveals on this machine through its own records.
.Pp
The config file is local to this machine, so the options and the
.Ar oracle
arguments take the form of
.Cm create .
Every machine of a vault must record the same ordered oracle list
and the same threshold.
A revocation from the plate derives the client keys of a machine
from this list, so a divergent list breaks it.
The config file takes the default pool size, and the ceremony makes
no pool.
.Pp
The registry of the index holds each provisioned machine name.
A name that the registry marks retired stops the ceremony, and the
report names a new machine name as the path.
A name of the registry with no config of that name in the vault
directory names another machine.
The ceremony then warns that it replaces the records of that
machine, and it reads a confirmation at the prompt
.Dq Replace the records of that machine? Type yes: .
The word
.Dq yes
alone confirms.
A config of another machine name in the vault directory stops the
ceremony, because two machines must not run from one machine-local
set.
.Pp
The ceremony can run again on a provisioned machine.
A re-run of the same command line verifies the passphrase at the
canary record of each live oracle that holds a seal on this machine,
before any enrollment.
The check walks the canaries one oracle at a time, in list order.
A canary that fails before any pass names the passphrase and the
record-side causes of that oracle, and the ceremony stops there.
It sends no request to a later canary, and no enrollment.
A canary that fails after a pass at another oracle enrolls again,
with a fresh seal and a fresh index wrap.
The re-run writes a dead index wrap again.
It enrolls the record of each slot that holds no wrap of this
machine at an oracle, and a record with a wrap stays as it is.
.Pp
A command line that changes the oracle list or the threshold is a
variant of the ceremony.
The ceremony writes the new config file before any enrollment, so a
stopped ceremony leaves a config that names the target state.
Run the same variant on each machine of the vault.
.Pp
An added oracle takes the next free position, and the loop enrolls
the new pairs.
A position takes the word
.Dq retired
in place of an oracle argument, and the ceremony deletes this
machine's wrap files, canary seal, and index wrap of that position.
The same deletion precedes a replacement of a position: a new static
public key and URL at the same index.
The report then names the
.Cm kit
subcommand, so the owner destroys this vault's records at the
departing oracle.
A retired position keeps its index, and no oracle takes it again.
The threshold must not exceed the count of the live positions.
.Pp
A record loss at a live position shows in no list change, because
the static public key and the URL of the position stay.
The three causes are a store wipe, a third strike at a record, and
a host restore from a backup.
A ceremony report that names a junk canary at one oracle after a
pass at another names the position to give.
The
.Fl x
option names such a position, and the option repeats for each one.
The ceremony deletes this machine's wrap files, canary seal, and
index wrap of each named position before any enrollment.
The loop then enrolls the records of that position again, and every
other position stays as it is.
A retired position stops the ceremony, and the
.Fl a
full run takes no
.Fl x
option, because it covers every position.
.Pp
A threshold change re-splits every share with the new threshold, and
it re-enrolls every record of this machine with a fresh mask.
It writes the change marker before the first record, and it removes
the marker after the last one.
An interrupted threshold change re-runs from the start under
.Cm provision .
.Pp
The
.Fl a
flag re-enrolls every record of this machine under one passphrase.
It removes the change marker of a stopped passphrase change, so it
is the recovery of a change that a lost oracle blocked.
The report names the removal.
.Pp
The ceremony refuses to start while the change marker exists, unless
it is the threshold re-run or the
.Fl a
full run.
The refusal names the
.Cm resume
subcommand for a passphrase change, and the
.Cm provision
subcommand for a threshold change.
.It Cm kit Op Fl m Ar machine
Print the revocation kit of one machine on the standard output.
The kit names the machine, each position of the config, live or
retired, and the record file name of each record of that machine
at that position.
An operator of a self-hosted oracle needs a record file name to
delete that record.
The kit holds no secret.
.Pp
The first line holds the word
.Dq machine ,
one space, and the machine name.
Each position then takes one line of the word
.Dq oracle ,
its index, and its URL.
A retired position takes the word
.Dq retired
in place of its URL, because the records of the machine stay at
the departing oracle.
One line of the word
.Dq record ,
the position, and the record file name follows for each record of
the machine at that position, and the canary record comes last.
.Pp
The subcommand derives the kit at each run, and no file of the
vault holds a kit.
A saved copy of the kit names no record of a later refill, so
derive the kit again before a revocation.
.Pp
A run without the
.Fl m
option prints the kit of this machine.
It reads the config file, the device factor, and the wrap files of
this machine, so it opens no session and it reads no passphrase.
Each wrap file names one record of this machine, so the kit holds
every slot of every refill on this machine.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl m Ar machine
Print the kit of the machine
.Ar machine ,
for a machine that is lost.
The subcommand takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 ,
it derives the device factor of that name, and it writes no file.
It opens the index of the vault under the index key of that plate.
The next free slot index of the index bounds the kit.
The kit of a named machine lists one record for each slot of the
vault.
A slot that the machine never enrolled names no record file at the
oracle.
A plate of another vault does not open the index, and the
subcommand stops with a report.
.El
.It Xo
.Cm revoke
.Op Fl r
.Fl m Ar machine
.Op Ar position ...
.Xc
Revoke the records of the machine
.Ar machine
at each chosen oracle, from the plate.
Run it on a surviving machine after the loss of that machine.
The subcommand takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 ,
it derives the client keys of that machine, and it opens the index
under the index key of that plate.
It opens no session, it reads no passphrase, and it needs no file
of the lost machine.
The records of the machine at one oracle are one record of each
slot of the vault, and the canary record, as
.Cm kit
lists them.
.Pp
Each
.Ar position
argument names one live position of the config file.
A run without an argument takes every live position.
A retired position holds no oracle, so it takes no request, and the
report says so.
.Pp
A run without the
.Fl r
option sends the lock: one wrong attempt per record, at the
counter value 4294967295.
That value passes the replay check against every lower stored
counter, so a raised counter cannot block the lock.
The attempt burns one strike, and the stored counter of the record
takes the highest value.
No later request of that record passes the replay check.
The record then answers junk to every caller, and every later
enrollment of it fails with an HTTP error.
The record keeps its file at the oracle.
.Pp
The lock retires the machine name at that oracle, because no
enrollment under that name passes there again.
The subcommand marks the name retired in the machine registry of
the index, and the mark never clears.
A run of
.Cm provision
under that name refuses, and a replacement machine takes a new
name.
.Pp
To deny the lost machine a quorum, lock its records at enough
oracles.
With
.Ar n
live oracles and the threshold
.Ar k ,
locks at
.Ar n No \- Ar k No + 1
of them deny every quorum.
The report states that count.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r
Send the replacement in place of the lock: one enrollment per
record, under fresh key material and a random pin secret, at the
same counter value.
The oracle replaces the key material of the record and resets the
stored counter.
Every wrap of the lost machine is then dead.
The replacement retires no name.
.El
.Pp
The report names each oracle where the lock or the replacement
landed, with the record count.
It names each oracle where a request failed, with the state of the
last failure, and the subcommand then exits 1.
It names each position that got no complete lock, so the owner
locks or deletes the remaining records of the machine there.
.Cm kit
names each record file for an operator.
The passphrase of a lost machine may be known, so the report
directs the owner to
.Cm passwd
on every other machine.
.Pp
No oracle operation freezes a record, and no oracle operation
reverses a lock or a replacement.
An operator of a self-hosted oracle can stop the service, and can
delete the record files that
.Cm kit
names.
.It Xo
.Cm recover
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl \-ceiling Ar ceiling
.Xc
Recover the entries of the vault from the plate.
The subcommand takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 .
It opens no session, it reads no passphrase, and it sends no oracle
request.
A recovered secret prints to the terminal, one entry at a time, and it
reaches no file.
.Pp
A vault directory that holds a shared set takes the plate-plus-files
path.
For each slot from 0 to the ceiling, the subcommand re-derives the
entry key, names the entry file by the hash of that key, and opens the
file of that name under the key.
It opens the index of the shared set under the index key of the plate,
and it names each recovered entry from the index.
It prints each entry name on the standard output, and each secret on
the terminal, in slot order.
A mnemonic entry prints as one QR code, in the UTF-8 half blocks of
.Xr fugupass-qr 1 .
A signer scans that code from the screen.
.Pp
A copy of the shared set restores by a file copy onto a machine that
keeps its own machine-local set.
The counters and the wraps of the receiving machine stay valid,
because they bind to that machine's records, and the copy does not
touch them.
A restore onto a fresh machine is a machine provisioning ceremony of
.Cm provision .
An entry that a machine creates after the backup date is absent from
the copy.
A derived entry re-derives from the plate, and a stored secret sealed
after the backup date does not return from this copy.
.Pp
The index of the copy can be stale.
A stale index degrades the entry names alone: every entry file still
matches by the hash of its key, and it still opens under that key.
The subcommand then names each entry by its file name.
A rewind of the machine-local set to an older copy leaves a later
enrollment unmatched, and that stale wrap recovers by a
.Cm provision
ceremony.
.Pp
A vault directory that holds no shared set takes the plate-alone path.
The subcommand re-derives the entry key of every slot from 0 to the
ceiling, and it re-materializes the two derived candidates of each
slot: a password and a child mnemonic.
It prints the two candidates of each slot on the terminal, and it
reports the scanned range on the standard output.
Each candidate prints as text, because a candidate is no entry.
A scan past the last used slot is safe and costs nothing, because the
derivation is deterministic.
Without the vault files, the entry names and the metadata are gone,
and a stored secret does not return.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl w
Print the words of a mnemonic entry as text, in place of the QR
code.
.It Fl \-ceiling Ar ceiling
The highest slot of the scan, above the default of 1024 slots.
The plate-alone path materializes the candidates of each slot up to
this bound, so a large ceiling takes a long scan.
.El
.It Cm verify
Verify that a plate decodes to the master of this vault.
The subcommand takes the master from a plate scan of
.Xr fugupass-scan 1 .
It re-derives the plate check value of that master, and it compares
the value with the
.Ic plate-check
line of the config file of this machine.
The check value is one-way, so the config file leaks nothing about
the master.
.Pp
The subcommand opens no session, it reads no passphrase, and it sends
no oracle request.
It touches no oracle record, and it reveals no secret.
On a match, it records the date of the day in the index, under the
index key of the plate, and
.Cm audit
reports that date.
A mismatch names a wrong plate or a damaged plate, and it writes no
file.
.It Cm ls
Print the name of each entry of the index, one name on each line.
The subcommand reads the open index, and it sends no entry request.
.It Cm show Op Fl w Ar name
Reveal the entry
.Ar name .
The subcommand prints the secret of the entry to the terminal, and
it prints each metadata field to the standard output.
A metadata line holds the field name, a colon, one space, and the
value.
.Pp
A mnemonic entry prints as one QR code, in the UTF-8 half blocks of
.Xr fugupass-qr 1 .
A signer scans that code from the screen.
The index holds the type of each entry, so the shape of the secret
stands before the reveal.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl w
Print the words of a mnemonic entry as text, in place of the QR
code.
.El
.It Xo
.Cm add
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl c Ar class
.Op Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Ar name
.Xc
Import a secret into the entry
.Ar name .
The subcommand reads the secret from the terminal, at the prompt
.Dq Secret: .
No argument and no environment variable carries a secret.
.Pp
A name that the index holds takes a rotation.
.Cm add
then seals the new secret in the slot of that entry, and it consumes
no slot of the pool.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl T Ar type
The type of the entry: password, mnemonic, passphrase, totp, note,
or shadow.
A new entry needs this option, and a rotation takes the type of the
entry from the index.
.It Fl c Ar class
The origin class of the entry: derived, stored, sovereign, or shadow.
The default is stored.
The type of the entry sets the classes that it takes, so a shadow
entry needs this option.
No file of the vault records the class, so the option reaches this
command alone.
.It Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
One metadata field of the entry.
The option can come more than one time, and each field name comes
one time only.
The type of the entry sets the field names that it takes.
.El
.It Xo
.Cm gen
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Ar name
.Xc
Make the derived entry
.Ar name
from a slot of the pool.
The entry takes the derived origin class, and the subcommand keeps
the candidate that the type needs.
.Pp
A name that the index holds takes a rotation.
.Cm gen
then consumes a new slot, it increments the version of the entry,
and it extends the slot list of the entry.
Each old version stays recoverable from the master plate.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl T Ar type
The type of the entry: password, mnemonic, or passphrase.
A new entry needs this option, and a rotation takes the type of the
entry from the index.
.It Fl f Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
One metadata field of the entry, as
.Cm add
takes it.
.El
.It Cm totp Ar name
Reveal the totp entry
.Ar name ,
compute the current code of it, and print the code to the terminal.
The computation runs on this machine, and it takes no request of its
own.
.It Cm audit
Print the state of the plate verification and of the shadow entries.
The first line holds
.Dq verified ,
a colon, one space, and the date of the last plate verification of
.Cm verify .
A vault with no verification prints no such line, and the report
names the absent date.
The subcommand then prints one line of each stale shadow entry: the
verification date of it, one space, and the entry name.
A shadow entry is stale when its verification date is older than the
audit age.
The config file holds that age as
.Ic audit-age ,
and the default is 365 days.
.Pp
The index holds the type of each entry, so the subcommand reveals
the shadow entries alone.
An entry of another type takes no oracle request.
.El
.Pp
.Cm add
and
.Cm gen
consume the lowest free slot of the pool that holds the wraps of
this machine at
.Ar threshold
live oracles or more.
Each one writes the index with the slot consumed, and it writes the
entry file after that.
The records and the wraps of the slot stay as they are.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width HOME
.It Ev HOME
The home directory of the default vault directory.
.El
.Sh FILES
The first path below is the default vault directory, and each other
path sits inside the vault directory.
.Bl -tag -width "machine/wrap.index.i" -compact
.It Pa ~/.fugupass
the vault directory of a run without the
.Fl d
option
.It Pa index
the sealed index of the vault
.It Pa machine/canary.\& Ns Ar i
the canary check seal of oracle
.Ar i
.It Pa machine/change
the marker of an incomplete passphrase change or threshold change
.It Pa machine/config
the config file of this machine, plaintext
.It Pa machine/counters
the last counter of each record of this machine
.It Pa machine/factor
the device factor of this machine
.It Pa machine/wrap.index.\& Ns Ar i
this machine's index wrap of oracle
.Ar i
.It Pa machine/wrap.\& Ns Ar e Ns Pa \&.\& Ns Ar i
this machine's wrap of slot
.Ar e
at oracle
.Ar i
.El
.Pp
The sealed file of one entry, and the sealed file of one free slot,
each take the lowercase hex of the hash of the slot key as the file
name.
A listing of the vault directory therefore names no entry.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success, and 1 when a subcommand fails.
A session exits 0 when the interface process exited 0, and 1 on every
other end of it.
An unknown subcommand and a wrong command line give the usage on the
standard error and the exit status 2.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Every report goes to the standard error.
A report of a failed request names the oracle and one of the four
states below.
The four states take four different user actions.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It the oracle answers an HTTP error
The oracle counted no attempt, and the request is retryable.
.It the transport fails
The oracle can have counted the attempt, because the answer can get
lost after the record changes.
.It the answer fails the authentication of the oracle
The static public key of the config file does not match the oracle
that answered.
Check the provisioning of that position, and the transport to it.
.It the answer is junk
The oracle answers junk for a wrong passphrase, for a missing or
wiped record, and for a counter violation.
The response bytes hold the cause of none of them.
.El
.Pp
The reports below name a state of the session.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It the canary of oracle Ar i : the check fails
The passphrase does not open the canary of that oracle, and the
report names the URL of it.
A failure at the first canary of the session names the passphrase
and the record-side causes of that oracle, because the two look
identical.
A failure after a pass at another oracle names the record-side
causes alone.
The session sends no entry request to that oracle.
.It the quorum takes Ar k No reachable oracles, and Ar m No answered
Fewer than
.Ar k
oracles are reachable.
The report names the state of each oracle, and the session performs
no reveal.
.It the index does not open, and the canary of each quorum oracle passed
The index file or one index wrap of this machine is wrong.
This failure is no junk answer, and the report names both causes.
.It the index stays closed: the quorum covers fewer than Ar k No live index wraps
The report names each dead or unreachable index wrap, and it names
the
.Cm provision
subcommand.
The session opens no entry name until the wraps come back.
.It slot Ar e : the entry of this quorum does not open
The reconstructed key does not open the entry file.
The failing oracle has no name, because the decrypt is the only junk
detector.
The session substitutes one place of the quorum and tries again.
.It slot Ar e No at oracle Ar i : the session sent two requests to this record
A decrypt failure names no oracle, so each record of the quorum can
hold the wrong pin.
The third wrong request destroys the key material of that record at
the oracle.
The session therefore stops the reveal of that slot.
.It slot Ar e : the session reveals it no other way
No untried reachable oracle remains, so the substitutions end.
.It the pool holds Ar n No free slots, at the low watermark of Ar w
The
.Cm refill
subcommand adds slots to the pool.
.Cm add
and
.Cm gen
still run.
.It the pool of this vault holds no free slot
.Cm add
and
.Cm gen
refuse a new entry.
The
.Cm refill
subcommand adds slots to the pool.
.It no free slot of the pool holds the wraps of this machine at Ar k No live oracles
Free slots remain, and no one of them reveals on this machine.
The
.Cm provision
subcommand writes the wraps of this machine.
.El
.Pp
The reports below name a state of the provisioning ceremony.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It this machine's records at oracle Ar i No are orphaned
A retirement or a replacement of a position deletes this machine's
files of that position.
The records at that oracle stay, so the report names the
.Cm kit
subcommand, and the owner destroys them there.
.It this ceremony re-enrolled every record of this machine and removed the change marker
The
.Fl a
full run, or the re-run of a threshold change, completed and cleared
the marker.
The vault reveals again.
.It the round count of this machine differs from the command line
A re-run takes the config of this machine, and this ceremony changes
no round count.
A list change that breaks the position rule, a list change under a
marker, and a change of the list and the threshold in one run each
stop the ceremony as well, and the report names the cause.
.El
.Pp
The reports below name a state of a revocation.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It the lock landed on Ar c No records of the machine Ar m No at oracle Ar i
Every request of the machine at that oracle got an answer, and the
records of it there answer junk from now on.
A replacement reports the same line with the word
.Dq replacement .
.It the lock reached Ar c No of Ar t No records of the machine Ar m No at oracle Ar i
A request failed at that oracle, and the next line names the state
of the last failure.
The records that no request reached stay as they were.
Run the subcommand again for that position.
.It the machine Ar m No is retired, and the registry of the index marks it
A lock landed at one oracle at least.
A replacement machine provisions under a new name.
.It the records of the machine Ar m No remain at oracle Ar i
That position got no complete lock.
Lock or delete the records there, and
.Cm kit
names each record file.
.It the registry of the index marks the machine Ar m No retired
The
.Cm provision
subcommand refuses a retired name.
Give a new machine name.
.El
.Pp
The report below names the state of a plate verification.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It the plate check value differs from the config file
The plate is a wrong plate, or a damaged plate.
The config file of this machine holds the check value of the plate of
this vault, and the subcommand wrote no date.
.El
